Quick update: after some quick tests it seems that `WithRequireLeader()` does not solve the issue with an active `Watch()` after the connected etcd endpoint becomes partitioned.

I think we have the following scenarios to consider:

1) Single node API server/etcd
2) Quorum-based cluster (e.g. 3 nodes) where K8s control plane **is colocated** with the etcd nodes (e.g. one API server and one etcd instance on one node)
3) Quorum-based cluster (e.g. 3 nodes) where K8s control plane **is not colocated** with the etcd endpoints (separate nodes for K8s control plane and etcd nodes)

We can rule out **(1)** because technically a partition with just one node in the cluster (K8s control plane and etcd) cannot happen.

**(3)** is mostly addressed with existing gRPC timeouts and keep-alives so when an API server connected to an etcd node that gets partitioned this API server will reconnect to other reachable etcd nodes (endpoints). However, this is only true if the API server itself is not within that partition (e.g. multi-zone deployment where a whole zone becomes partitioned; read on for my observations).

**(2)** is the interesting one: an API server is connected to an etcd node and both become partitioned from the majority. But the API server has active watches against this etcd node and serves these (Kubernetes) watches to Kubernetes clients (controllers, etc.). I was expecting that the `WithRequireLeader()` gets evaluated even after establishing the `Watch()`. But it seems my assumption is not correct, i.e. it is only evaluated (checked) when `Watch()` is called. At least I did not see an existing `Watch()` being dropped after partitioning the nodes from the quorum (I did check the underlying gRPC transport status and it remained in `"CONNECTING"` stayed for as long as the partition was not resolved.

So what does that mean for the issue raised here? One could argue that if more than just one etcd node is partitioned, i.e. also the API server and perhaps other Kubernetes controllers running in that partitioned environment (e.g. a zone), it makes no difference using `WithRequireLeader()` because:

- the clients (custom controllers, etc.) are also partitioned and thus cannot connect to any other API server anyways; would be nice to return an error here instead of hanging but the system won't self-heal during the partition
- even if the clients (custom controllers) would not be in that partition, the corresponding API server could not be reached anymore and client-go session handling should address this (e.g. reconnect to another API server)

@jingyih you write:

> It is correct that without WithRequireLeader, etcd server will not cancel watch stream when itself is isolated from the cluster

I did not see an existing watch being dropped, e.g. using this simple `clientv3` code:

```go
	leaderCtx := clientv3.WithRequireLeader(ctx)
	// rch := cli.Watch(context.Background(), "", clientv3.WithPrefix(), clientv3.WithRev(0+1), clientv3.WithPrevKV())

	fmt.Println("starting watch")
	rch := cli.Watch(leaderCtx, "", clientv3.WithPrefix(), clientv3.WithRev(rev+1))
	for wresp := range rch {
		...
		}
	}
```

Once I partition the etcd node including this client it will not drop the `Watch()`. Looking at the definition of `WithRequireLeader()` it seems that this is performed only during establishing the `Watch()` but not reevaluated as long as the client has a successful connection to the etcd node (e.g. node is partitioned but not dead).

```go
// WithRequireLeader requires client requests to only succeed
// when the cluster has a leader.
func WithRequireLeader(ctx context.Context) context.Context {
	md, ok := metadata.FromOutgoingContext(ctx)
	if !ok { // no outgoing metadata ctx key, create one
		md = metadata.Pairs(rpctypes.MetadataRequireLeaderKey, rpctypes.MetadataHasLeader)
		return metadata.NewOutgoingContext(ctx, md)
	}
	copied := md.Copy() // avoid racey updates
	// overwrite/add 'hasleader' key/value
	metadataSet(copied, rpctypes.MetadataRequireLeaderKey, rpctypes.MetadataHasLeader)
	return metadata.NewOutgoingContext(ctx, copied)
}
```
https://github.com/etcd-io/etcd/blob/67da93f739e33ee9b34792fccba2d0c100264ea4/clientv3/ctx.go#L26

Since I am by no means an expert on the etcd `Watch()` semantics here, I might be doing something wrong. It would be great to get some clarification whether this issue (I raised, I know :) ...) needs to be addressed, documented, or can actually be addressed at all with the current implementation of `WithRequireLeader()`.

https://godoc.org/github.com/etcd-io/etcd/clientv3#Watcher has some detailed watch behaviors.

```
type Watcher interface {
    // Watch watches on a key or prefix. The watched events will be returned
    // through the returned channel. If revisions waiting to be sent over the
    // watch are compacted, then the watch will be canceled by the server, the
    // client will post a compacted error watch response, and the channel will close.
    // If the requested revision is 0 or unspecified, the returned channel will
    // return watch events that happen after the server receives the watch request.
    // If the context "ctx" is canceled or timed out, returned "WatchChan" is closed,
    // and "WatchResponse" from this closed channel has zero events and nil "Err()".
    // The context "ctx" MUST be canceled, as soon as watcher is no longer being used,
    // to release the associated resources.
    //
    // If the context is "context.Background/TODO", returned "WatchChan" will
    // not be closed and block until event is triggered, except when server
    // returns a non-recoverable error (e.g. ErrCompacted).
    // For example, when context passed with "WithRequireLeader" and the
    // connected server has no leader (e.g. due to network partition),
    // error "etcdserver: no leader" (ErrNoLeader) will be returned,
    // and then "WatchChan" is closed with non-nil "Err()".
    // In order to prevent a watch stream being stuck in a partitioned node,
    // make sure to wrap context with "WithRequireLeader".
    //
    // Otherwise, as long as the context has not been canceled or timed out,
    // watch will retry on other recoverable errors forever until reconnected.
    //
    // TODO: explicitly set context error in the last "WatchResponse" message and close channel?
    // Currently, client contexts are overwritten with "valCtx" that never closes.
    // TODO(v3.4): configure watch retry policy, limit maximum retry number
    // (see https://github.com/etcd-io/etcd/issues/8980)
    Watch(ctx context.Context, key string, opts ...OpOption) WatchChan
```

